.TH CHECK_KERNEL 1 "12 November 2026" "Check Kernel v2.0.25" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
check_kernel \- download, configure, build, and install Linux kernels
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B check_kernel.sh
.RI [ CPU ] [ OPTION ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B check_kernel.sh
checks kernel.org for the newest release in the current kernel tree, downloads Linux kernel source, opens the kernel configuration, builds the kernel and modules, installs them below
.B /boot
and
.B /lib/modules,
and updates the GRUB configuration. It must normally be run as root on a Linux system.
.PP
Sources are stored below
.B /usr/src/linux-VERSION-CPU
and temporary downloads below
.B /tmp/linuxkrntmp.
The script asks for confirmation before downloading and installing a kernel.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -A , --amd
Build for AMD processors. This may be combined with the options below.
.TP
.BR -I , -i , --intel
Build for Intel processors. This may be combined with the options below.
.TP
.BI -t  TREE
Resolve and build the newest release in a kernel tree, for example
.BR "-t 6.12" .
.TP
.BI -k  VERSION
Build an exact release, for example
.BR "-k 6.12.90" .
.TP
.BR -m
Use the currently running kernel version, restore its boot configuration, run
.B olddefconfig
and open
.B menuconfig.
.TP
.BR -l , --list
List available kernel branches. With
.B -A
or
.B -I,
the selected release can also be built.
.TP
.B --clean
Run
.B make clean
before building.
.TP
.B --change-cpu
For a cross-CPU build, update the boot symlinks to make the new CPU target the default. Without this option, cross-CPU builds leave the current boot selection unchanged.
.TP
.BR -h , --help
Show usage information.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
sudo ./check_kernel.sh --help
sudo ./check_kernel.sh --list
sudo ./check_kernel.sh -A -t 6.12
sudo ./check_kernel.sh --intel -k 6.12.90 --clean
.EE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
The commands
.BR curl , wget , tar , make , grep , cut , sed , awk , uname , nproc , update-initramfs
and
.B update-grub
must be available. The optional
.B pv
command displays extraction progress. Network access to
.B https://www.kernel.org
is required for release discovery and downloads.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /boot/config-*
Kernel configurations used as build defaults.
.TP
.B /tmp/linuxkrntmp
Temporary kernel archives; removed on exit.
.SH WARNINGS
Kernel installation modifies
.B /boot,
.B /lib/modules,
and boot symlinks, and may affect the next reboot. Review the selected version and configuration before answering
.B yes.
The script currently downloads archives without verifying a separately supplied checksum or signature; use an appropriate trusted network and verify releases independently for high-assurance deployments.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero after a successful listing, configuration/build/install, or a deliberate non-update response. Returns non-zero for missing dependencies, invalid arguments, failed downloads/builds, or missing configuration.
.SH AUTHOR
Check Kernel was authored and maintained by FNH media KG.
